.TH KEEL\-TRANSITION 8 "2026-09-26" "keel-transition 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
keel\-transition \- move a TurnKey Linux 19.0 appliance onto the Keel Linux archive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B keel\-transition
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B keel\-transition \-\-apply
.RI [ \-\-force\-unsigned ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B keel\-transition \-\-rollback
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B keel\-transition
surveys a running TurnKey Linux 19.0 appliance, writes the Keel instance
spec that describes it, and switches its apt configuration over to the
Keel Linux archive. It works in three phases. Each one is reversible and
each one reports every file it touches, by name, before or after it
touches it.
.SS Phase 1: survey (the default)
Runs
.BR keel (8)
.BR inspect ,
writes
.I /etc/keel/instance.yaml
when that file is absent (an existing spec is never rewritten), writes the
field by field report to
.IR /var/lib/keel/transition/survey\-report.txt ,
prints every field inspect could not infer, and prints the three changes
.B \-\-apply
would make. Nothing under
.I /etc/apt
is changed and no root privilege is needed.
.SS Phase 2: \-\-apply
Writes
.I /etc/apt/sources.list.d/keel.sources
(deb822,
.B Signed\-By
.IR /usr/share/keyrings/keel\-archive\-keyring.gpg ,
suite
.BR trixie ,
component
.BR main ),
writes
.I /etc/apt/preferences.d/keel
with
.B Pin: release o=Keel Linux
at priority
.BR 1001 ,
and disables the upstream
.I /etc/apt/sources.list.d/turnkey.list
by renaming it to
.IR turnkey.list.disabled\-by\-keel .
The list is renamed, never deleted, so phase 3 can put the original bytes
back. Running
.B \-\-apply
twice leaves byte identical files.
.PP
.I turnkey.list
is the name an appliance of the 18.x lineage carries. An appliance
installed from the 19.0 media has none: its upstream archive lives in
.IR /etc/apt/sources.list.d/*.sources ,
deb822, in the same file as the Debian stanzas. Renaming one of those
would disable Debian with it, so
.B keel\-transition
leaves them alone and names them in its report instead. They do no harm:
the origin pin at 1001 makes the Keel archive win over them whatever
else is configured.
.SS Phase 3: \-\-rollback
Undoes exactly those three changes and nothing else: the upstream list is
renamed back, byte for byte, and the source and the pin are removed. A
file at either of those two paths that
.B keel\-transition
did not write is left alone and reported, and the run exits 7. The
instance spec is left in place: it is the operator's, and removing it is
the operator's decision.
.SH IT NEVER TOUCHES A PACKAGE
.B keel\-transition
does not run
.BR apt\-get ,
and it installs, upgrades and removes nothing. It changes apt sources,
one preferences file and the instance spec. After
.B \-\-apply
the operator runs
.B apt\-get update
and decides what to install. That separation is deliberate: a transition
that also upgraded would be two failures in one command, and only one of
them would be reversible by
.BR \-\-rollback .
.SH THE UNSIGNED ARCHIVE
Before it changes anything,
.B \-\-apply
fetches
.I dists/<suite>/InRelease
from the archive over IPv6, or
.I Release
and
.IR Release.gpg ,
and verifies the signature with
.BR gpgv (1)
against
.IR /usr/share/keyrings/keel\-archive\-keyring.gpg .
When no signature verifies, it refuses, changes nothing and exits 4. It
checks; it does not assume.
.PP
.B \-\-force\-unsigned
overrides the refusal. It prints a warning naming the risk and writes the
source with
.BR "Trusted: yes" ,
which turns apt's signature checking off for that archive: anything that
can answer for that name, and any proxy on the way, can then install code
that runs as root on the appliance. Use it on a test bench, never on a
live one.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Phase 2.
.TP
.B \-\-rollback
Phase 3.
.TP
.B \-\-force\-unsigned
Enable an archive whose Release does not verify. Test benches only.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " DIR"
Work on this filesystem tree instead of
.IR / .
Every path above is read and written under
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BI \-\-archive\-uri " URL"
Default
.IR https://archive.keellinux.org .
.TP
.BI \-\-suite " NAME"
Default
.IR trixie .
.TP
.BI \-\-components " LIST"
Default
.IR main .
.TP
.BI \-\-spec " FILE"
Default
.IR /etc/keel/instance.yaml .
.TP
.BI \-\-report " FILE"
Default
.IR /var/lib/keel/transition/survey\-report.txt .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-inspect
Do not run
.BR keel ;
survey the apt state only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including every no-op case.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error.
.TP
.B 2
.B \-\-apply
or
.B \-\-rollback
on the live system by a user other than root. Nothing was changed.
.TP
.B 3
.B keel inspect
could not infer a field the first boot would have asked for. The spec was
written with what was found.
.TP
.B 4
The archive has no Release that the keyring verifies. Nothing was changed.
.TP
.B 5
A file could not be written, or
.B \-\-apply
found a file it did not write at one of its two paths.
.TP
.B 6
.B keel\-archive\-keyring
is not installed, so nothing could be verified. Nothing was changed.
.TP
.B 7
.B \-\-rollback
left something behind, and said what.
.TP
.B 8
The
.B keel
command is not installed, so no spec was written.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/apt/sources.list.d/keel.sources
The deb822 source, written by phase 2.
.TP
.I /etc/apt/preferences.d/keel
The origin pin, written by phase 2.
.TP
.I /etc/apt/sources.list.d/turnkey.list
The upstream list, renamed by phase 2 and restored by phase 3.
.TP
.I /etc/keel/instance.yaml
The instance spec, written by phase 1 when it is absent.
.TP
.I /var/lib/keel/transition/survey\-report.txt
The field by field report of the last survey.
.TP
.I /usr/share/keyrings/keel\-archive\-keyring.gpg
The archive key, from
.BR keel\-archive\-keyring .
.SH EXAMPLES
Survey a Core 19.0 appliance, changing nothing:
.PP
.EX
keel\-transition
.EE
.PP
Apply against the project archive:
.PP
.EX
keel\-transition \-\-apply
apt\-get update
.EE
.PP
Apply against an unsigned staging archive on a test bench, over IPv6:
.PP
.EX
keel\-transition \-\-apply \-\-force\-unsigned \e
    \-\-archive\-uri 'http://[2804:710:d0:5:bb3f:380a:f07b:7951]:8081' \e
    \-\-suite trixie\-staging
.EE
.PP
Undo it:
.PP
.EX
keel\-transition \-\-rollback
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR keel (8),
.BR apt_preferences (5),
.BR sources.list (5),
.BR gpgv (1).
.PP
https://github.com/keel\-linux/keel\-transition
